      "side": 1,
      "message": "Throughout the code, you frequently convert POD (Plain Old Data) structs into `std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e` for USB transmission:\n\n```c\n// In cli.cc\nauto params_span \u003d std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e(reinterpret_cast\u003cconst uint8_t*\u003e(\u0026params), sizeof(params));\n\n// In ft_scsi.cc\nauto cbw_span \u003d std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e(reinterpret_cast\u003cconst uint8_t*\u003e(\u0026cbw), sizeof(cbw));\n\n```\n\nC++20 introduced `std::as_bytes(std::span{...})`, but it returns `std::byte`, whereas your USB layer correctly uses `uint8_t`. Instead of scattering `reinterpret_cast` everywhere, you can centralize this into a pair of highly safe, universally usable templates.\n\n**Add these to `util/focaltech/ft_util.h`:**\n\n```c\n// Safely view any TriviallyCopyable object as a read-only byte span\ntemplate \u003ctypename T\u003e\n  requires std::is_trivially_copyable_v\u003cT\u003e\ninline std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e AsUint8Span(const T\u0026 obj) {\n  return {reinterpret_cast\u003cconst uint8_t*\u003e(\u0026obj), sizeof(T)};\n}\n\n// Safely view any TriviallyCopyable object as a mutable byte span\ntemplate \u003ctypename T\u003e\n  requires std::is_trivially_copyable_v\u003cT\u003e\ninline std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e AsWritableUint8Span(T\u0026 obj) {\n  return {reinterpret_cast\u003cuint8_t*\u003e(\u0026obj), sizeof(T)};\n}\n\n```\n\nNow, your protocol parsing becomes beautifully clean:\n\n* In `cli.cc`: `auto params_span \u003d AsUint8Span(params);`  \n* In `ft_scsi.cc`: `auto cbw_span \u003d AsUint8Span(cbw);`  \n* In `ft_scsi.cc`: `auto csw_span \u003d AsWritableUint8Span(csw);`",
