      "side": 1,
      "message": "**The Issue:**\nIn `util/focaltech/updater.cc`, the function `IsEcBinHasConfigPage` reads the configuration header from the firmware file and then evaluates whether the magic string matches.\n\n```cpp\n  FtConfigPage config{};\n  file.read(reinterpret_cast\u003cchar*\u003e(\u0026config), sizeof(config));\n  if (!file.good() \u0026\u0026 !file.eof()) {\n    FT_LOGE(\"Failed to read config page\");\n    return std::unexpected(FtError::kResultFail);\n  }\n\n  if (config.code_valid_control_word !\u003d kCodeValidMagic) {\n    return false;\n  }\n\n  if (file.gcount() !\u003d sizeof(config)) {\n    FT_LOGE(\"Failed to read config page (file too small).\");\n    return std::unexpected(FtError::kResultFail);\n  }\n\n```\n\nWhile initializing with `FtConfigPage config{};` prevents undefined behavior by zeroing the memory first, it is still logically backwards to inspect the parsed data (`code_valid_control_word`) *before* you\u0027ve confirmed that the file actually contained enough bytes to populate the structure (`file.gcount() !\u003d sizeof(config)`).\n\n**The Fix:**\nSimply swap the order of the checks so that you validate the size of the read before evaluating the payload.\n\n**Updated Code:**\n\n```cpp\n  FtConfigPage config{};\n  file.read(reinterpret_cast\u003cchar*\u003e(\u0026config), sizeof(config));\n  if (!file.good() \u0026\u0026 !file.eof()) {\n    FT_LOGE(\"Failed to read config page\");\n    return std::unexpected(FtError::kResultFail);\n  }\n\n  if (file.gcount() !\u003d sizeof(config)) {\n    FT_LOGE(\"Failed to read config page (file too small).\");\n    return std::unexpected(FtError::kResultFail);\n  }\n\n  if (config.code_valid_control_word !\u003d kCodeValidMagic) {\n    return false;\n  }\n\n```",
