      "message": "### Rationale\n\nIn C++, the **Strict Aliasing Rule** allows the compiler\u0027s optimizer to assume that pointers of completely different types (like `uint8_t*` and `BulkCbWrap*`) will never point to the same memory location.\n\nWhen you use `reinterpret_cast` to cast a raw byte array directly into a struct pointer:\n\n```cpp\nconst auto* cbw \u003d reinterpret_cast\u003cconst BulkCbWrap*\u003e(data.data());\n\n```\n\nYou are violating this rule. In unoptimized builds, this usually works fine. However, when compiling with optimizations enabled (`-O2` or `-O3`), the compiler may aggressively reorder, cache, or completely optimize away the memory reads because it assumes nothing is mutating the struct\u0027s underlying memory. This causes unpredictable \"heisenbugs\" where tests randomly fail in release builds.\n\n**The Solution:**\n\n1. **`std::memcpy`**: For moving data from a pointer (`data.data()`) into a struct, `memcpy` is the standard-compliant approach. Modern compilers recognize this pattern and optimize it into zero-cost register reads—no actual function call is made.\n2. **`std::bit_cast`**: For converting between two types that are the exact same size at compile-time (like the 16-byte `cbw.cmd` array to the 16-byte `FtCmdFormat` struct), C++20\u0027s `std::bit_cast` is the safest, most idiomatic tool for type-punning.\n\n---\n\n### The Change\n\n**File:** `util/focaltech/updater_test.cc`\n**Location:** Inside `StubUsbTransport::Send()`\n\n**Remove this block:**\n\n```cpp\n        // Use structured packet inspection instead of magic indices.\n        if (data.size() \u003d\u003d sizeof(BulkCbWrap)) {\n          const auto* cbw \u003d reinterpret_cast\u003cconst BulkCbWrap*\u003e(data.data());\n          // Ensure the CBW contains an FocalTech command (opcode 0xDC).\n          if (cbw-\u003ecmd[0] \u003d\u003d kFtCmdAction) {\n            const auto* ft_cmd \u003d reinterpret_cast\u003cconst FtCmdFormat*\u003e(cbw-\u003ecmd);\n            if (ft_cmd-\u003esubcmd \u003d\u003d kFtSubcmdErasePage) {\n              erased_addresses_.push_back(ToLittleEndian(ft_cmd-\u003eaddress));\n            }\n          }\n        }\n\n```\n\n**Replace with this block:**\n\n```cpp\n        // Use structured packet inspection instead of magic indices.\n        if (data.size() \u003d\u003d sizeof(BulkCbWrap)) {\n          // 1. Use memcpy because data.data() is a pointer, preventing bit_cast.\n          BulkCbWrap cbw;\n          std::memcpy(\u0026cbw, data.data(), sizeof(cbw));\n\n          // Ensure the CBW contains a FocalTech command (opcode 0xDC).\n          if (cbw.cmd[0] \u003d\u003d kFtCmdAction) {\n            // 2. Use bit_cast because cbw.cmd (uint8_t[16]) and FtCmdFormat are exactly 16 bytes.\n            auto ft_cmd \u003d std::bit_cast\u003cFtCmdFormat\u003e(cbw.cmd);\n            \n            if (ft_cmd.subcmd \u003d\u003d kFtSubcmdErasePage) {\n              erased_addresses_.push_back(ToLittleEndian(ft_cmd.address));\n            }\n          }\n        }\n\n```\n\n*(Note: If you have already applied the C++ namespace refactor we discussed earlier, remember to drop the `Ft` prefix from `FtCmdFormat`, `kFtCmdAction`, and `kFtSubcmdErasePage` in your updated code!)*",
